'''Lalande 21185''' (also known as '''BD+36 2147''', '''Gliese 411''', and '''HD 95735'''<ref name="SIMBAD">{{cite simbad
 | title=HD 95735
 | access-date=2009-05-29}}</ref>) is a star in the south of [[Ursa Major]]. It is the [[apparent magnitude|apparent]] brightest [[red dwarf]] in the northern hemisphere.<ref group=nb>Only [[AX Microscopii]] and [[Lacaille 9352]], in the southern hemisphere, are brighter</ref><ref>{{cite web
 | url =http://www.universetoday.com/111716/14-red-dwarf-stars-to-view-with-backyard-telescopes/
 | title =14 Red Dwarf Stars to View with Backyard Telescopes
 | last =Dickinson
 | first =David
 | date =2015-12-23
 | publisher =Universe Today
 | access-date =2016-12-04}}</ref><ref>{{cite web
 | url =http://kencroswell.com/thebrightestreddwarf.html
 | title =The Brightest Red Dwarf
 | last =Croswell
 | first =Ken
 | date =July 2002
 | website =KenCroswell.com
 | access-date =2016-12-04}}</ref> Despite this, and being relatively close by, it is very dim (as are all red dwarfs), being only [[apparent magnitude|magnitude]] 7.5 in [[visible light]] and thus too faint to be seen with the unaided eye. The [[star]] is visible through a [[small telescope]] or [[binoculars]].<ref name=sherrod_koed2003/>

At {{Convert|8.304|ly|pc|abbr=off|lk=on}}<ref name="Gaia3"/> away it is one of the [[List of nearest stars|stars nearest]] to the Solar System; only the [[Alpha Centauri]] system, [[Barnard's Star]], [[Wolf 359]], and the [[brown dwarf]]s [[Luhman 16]] and [[WISE 0855−0714]] are known to be closer.<ref name="Reylé2021"/> Because of its proximity it is a frequent subject for [[astronomical surveys]] and other research and thus is known by numerous other designations, most commonly Gliese 411 and HD 95735. In approximately 19,900 years it will be at its closest, about 4.65 [[light-year|ly]] (1.43 [[parsec|pc]]) from the Sun, just over half its present distance.<ref name="García-Sánchez2001"/><ref name="SIMBAD2">{{cite web
 | url=http://cdsannotations.u-strasbg.fr/annotations/simbadObject/1764001
 | title=Annotations on HD 95735 object 
 | publisher=Centre de données astronomiques de Strasbourg
 | access-date=2010-04-14 }}</ref>

Lalande 21185 has two known [[exoplanet]]s and one candidate exoplanet, making it the [[list of nearest exoplanets|third closest]] confirmed [[planetary system]] to the Solar System.

== History ==
[[File:Near-stars-past-future-en.svg|left|thumb|Distances of the [[List of nearest stars|nearest stars]] from estimated 20,000 years ago until 80,000 years in the future]]

French astronomer [[Jérôme Lalande]] published the [[celestial coordinates]] of Lalande 21185 in the popular star catalog ''[[Histoire céleste française]]'' during year 1801. The catalog sequence numbers for majority of the observed stars, including Lalande 21185, were introduced in its 1847 edition by [[Francis Baily]].<ref>[[Francis Baily|Baily, Francis]]; [[Joseph Jérôme Le Français de Lalande|Lalande, Joseph Jérôme Le Français de]] (1847). "Catalogue of those stars in the Histoire céleste française of Jérôme Delalande, for which tables of reduction to the epoch 1800 habe been published by Prof. Schumacher". ''London (1847)''. [[Bibcode]]:[http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1847cshc.book.....B 1847cshc.book.....B]. [[Google Books|Google Books id]]: [https://books.google.com/books?id=oc0-AAAAcAAJ oc0-AAAAcAAJ].
</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |title=A catalogue of those stars in the "Histoire céleste française" of J. De Lalande for which tables of reduction to the session define format EPOCH1 = 1800 have been published by Professor Schumacher. |journal=British Ass. Adv. Sci |volume=1847 |author=Baily, F. |date=1847 |bibcode=1950Lalan1847....0B}}</ref> Today this star is one of just a few that are still commonly referred to by their Lalande catalog number.<ref>[http://www.thomaspynchon.com/mason-dixon/extra/lalande.html Joseph-Jérôme de Lalande] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090721084438/http://www.thomaspynchon.com/mason-dixon/extra/lalande.html |date=July 21, 2009 }}</ref>

In May 1857, [[Friedrich Wilhelm Argelander]] discovered the high [[proper motion]] of the star. It was sometimes called "Argelander's second star".<ref name="Lynn1872">{{cite journal  |last=Lynn |first=W. T. |title=On the Parallax and Proper Motion of Lalande 21185 |date=1872 |journal=[[Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society]] |volume=33 | pages=52–54 |bibcode=1872MNRAS..33...52L |doi=10.1093/mnras/33.1.52|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name="Winnecke1858">{{cite journal |last=Winnecke |first=A. |title=Über die Parallaxe des zweiten Argelander'schen Sterns, von Herrn Prof. Winnecke |date=1858 |journal=[[Astronomische Nachrichten]] |volume=48 | issue=1147 | pages=289–292 |bibcode=1858AN.....48..289W|doi = 10.1002/asna.18580481903 |url=https://zenodo.org/record/1424655}}</ref><ref name="Winnecke1872">{{cite journal |last=Winnecke |first=Friedrich August Theodor |title=Bestimmung der parallaxe des zweiten Argelander-'schen sternes aus messungen AM heliometer der sternwarte zu Bonn in den jahren 1857–1858 |date=1872 |journal=Leipzig, W. Engelmann |series=Astronomische Gesellschaft, Leipzig. Publication11|publisher=Leipzig |bibcode=1872bpza.book.....W|hdl=2027/nnc1.cu50717758 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> (The "first Argelander's star" is [[Groombridge 1830]], whose high proper motion was discovered by Argelander earlier—in 1842).  A large proper motion was recognised as an indicator for stars which lay close to the Sun, leading Argelander to request that its [[parallax]] be measured.

As per this request, [[Friedrich August Theodor Winnecke]] made the first measurement of the star's [[parallax]] of 0.511 [[Minute and second of arc|arc seconds]] between 1857–58 and thus first identifying Lalande 21185 as the second-closest-known star to the [[Sun]], after the [[Alpha Centauri]] system.<ref name="Winnecke1858" /> Since that time better measurements have placed the star farther away, but it remained the second-closest-known star system until the  [[astrophotography|astrophotographic]] discovery of two dim red dwarfs, Wolf 359 and Barnard's Star, in the early 20th century.<ref>{{ cite journal
 |title=The parallax of Lalande 21185 and γ Virginis from photographs taken at the Cambridge Observatory
 |bibcode=1905MNRAS..65..787R
 |author=Russell, H. N.
 |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society
 |volume=65
 |issue=8
 |date=June 1905
 |pages=787–800
 |doi=10.1093/mnras/65.8.787
 |url=https://zenodo.org/record/1431875
 |doi-access=free
 }}</ref>

==Properties==
[[File:NSV18593LightCurve.png|thumb|left|An X-ray [[light curve]] for a flare on NSV 18593, adapted from Pye ''et al.'' (2015)<ref name="Pye"/>]]
[[File:Angular map of fusors around Sol within 9ly (large).png|thumb|upright=1.5|The position of Lalande 21185 on a [[Plan position indicator|radar]] map among all stellar objects or [[star system|stellar systems]] within 9 light years (ly) from the map's center, the Sun (Sol). The diamond-shapes are their positions entered according to [[right ascension]] in [[hour angle|hours angle]] (indicated at the edge of the map's reference disc), and according to their [[declination]]. The second mark shows each's distance from Sol, with the [[Concentric objects|concentric]] circles indicating the distance in steps of one ly.]]
Lalande 21185 is a typical [[stellar classification|type-M]] [[main sequence|main-sequence]] star ([[red dwarf]]) with about 39% of the mass and radius of the Sun. It is also much cooler than the Sun with a surface temperature of 3,550 K. With just 2.2% of the Sun's luminosity,<ref name=Pineda2021/> it is intrinsically dim with an [[absolute magnitude]] of 10.48, emitting most of its energy in the [[infrared]].<ref name=johnson/> The proportion of elements other than hydrogen and helium is estimated based on the ratio of iron to hydrogen in the star when compared to the Sun. The logarithm of this ratio is −0.20, indicating that the proportion of iron is about 10<sup>−0.20</sup>, or 63% of the Sun. The surface gravity of this relatively compact star is approximately 65 times greater than the gravity at Earth's surface (log g = 4.8 cgs),<ref name=Fe/> which is more than twice the surface gravity of the Sun.

Lalande 21185 is listed as a [[BY Draconis variable|BY Draconis]] type variable star in the [[General Catalogue of Variable Stars]]. It is identified by the variable star designation NSV 18593.<ref name=gcvs>{{ cite web
| url=http://www.sai.msu.su/groups/cluster/gcvs/cgi-bin/search.cgi?search=NSV+18593
| title=NSV 18593
| work=[[General Catalogue of Variable Stars]], [[Sternberg Astronomical Institute]], Moscow, Russia
| access-date=2009-09-09}}</ref> Several star catalogs, including [[SIMBAD]], also classify it as a [[flare star]]. This conclusion is not supported by the primary reference these catalogs all use. The observations made in this reference show that it is rather quiet in comparison to other stars of its variable type.<ref>{{cite journal
| title=Discovery and observation of BY Draconis variables
| journal=[[Astrophysical Journal]] | issue=1 | volume=249 | date=October 1, 1981 | pages=210–217 
| doi=10.1086/159277 | bibcode=1981ApJ...249..210B
|author1=Bopp, B. W. |author2=Noah, P. V. |author3=Klimke, A. |author4=Africano, J. | doi-access=free }}</ref>

Lalande 21185 emits X-rays, and X-ray flares have been observed.<ref name=Schmitt>{{ cite journal |author1=Schmitt JHMM |author2=Fleming TA |author3=Giampapa MS |title=The X-Ray View of the Low-Mass Stars in the Solar Neighborhood |journal=Astrophys. J. |date=September 1995 |volume=450 |issue=9 |pages=392–400 |bibcode=1995ApJ...450..392S |doi=10.1086/176149 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name="Pye"/>

==Planetary system==

Data published in 2017 from the HIRES system at the [[W. M. Keck Observatory|Keck Observatory]] on [[Mauna Kea]] supported the existence of a close-in planet with an orbital period of just 9.8693±0.0016 days, being at least {{Earth mass|sym=y|3.8|link=yes}}.<ref name="Butler et al">{{cite journal | display-authors=2 | last1=Butler | first1=R. Paul | last2=Vogt | first2=Steven S. | last3=Laughlin | first3=Gregory | last4=Burt | first4=Jennifer A. | last5=Rivera | first5=Eugenio J. | last6=Tuomi | first6=Mikko | last7=Teske | first7=Johanna | last8=Arriagada | first8=Pamela | last9=Diaz | first9=Matias | last10=Holden | first10=Brad | last11=Keiser | first11=Sandy | author-link1=R. Paul Butler |author-link2=Steven S. Vogt | arxiv=1702.03571 | title=The LCES HIRES/Keck Precision Radial Velocity Exoplanet Survey |date=12 February 2017 | doi=10.3847/1538-3881/aa66ca | volume=153 | issue=5 | journal=The Astronomical Journal | page=208|bibcode = 2017AJ....153..208B | s2cid=14954371 | doi-access=free }}</ref>
Further radial velocity research with the [[SOPHIE échelle spectrograph]] and review of the original signal found that the 9.9 day period was undetectable, and instead proposed, using both datasets, an exoplanet orbiting the star with a period of either 12.95 or 1.08 days, much more likely 12.95, insofar as 1-day-period exoplanets seem to be rare in systems. This would give the planet a minimum mass of 2.99 Earth masses. It is too close to the star, and so therefore too hot, to be in the habitable zone, at all points within its eccentric orbit.<ref name="2019exoplanet">{{cite journal |doi=10.1051/0004-6361/201935019  |doi-access=free|last1=Díaz |first1=Rodrigo F. |last2=Delfosse |first2=Xavier |last3=Hobson |first3=Melissa J. |last4=Boisse |first4=Isabelle |last5=Astudillo-Defru |first5=Nicola |last6=Bonfils |first6=Xavier |last7=Henry |first7=Gregory W. |last8=Arnold |first8=Luc |last9=Bouchy |first9=François |last10=Bourrier |first10=Vincent |last11=Brugger |first11=Bastien |last12=Dalal |first12=Shweta |last13=Deleuil |first13=Magali |last14=Demangeon |first14=Olivier D. S. |last15=Dolon |first15=François |last16=Dumusque |first16=Xavier |last17=Forveille |first17=Thierry |last18=Hara |first18=Nathan C. |last19=Hébrard |first19=Guillaume |last20=Kiefer |first20=Flavien |last21=Lopez |first21=Théo |last22=Mignon |first22=Lucile |last23=Moreau |first23=François |last24=Mousis |first24=Olivier |last25=Moutou |first25=Claire |last26=Pepe |first26=Francesco |last27=Perruchot |first27=Sandrine |last28=Richaud |first28=Yoann |last29=Santerne |first29=Alexandre |last30=Santos |first30=Nuno C. |last31=Sottile |first31=Rico |last32=Stalport |first32=Manu |last33=Ségransan |first33=Damien |last34=Udry |first34=Stéphane |last35=Unger |first35=Nicolas |last36=Wilson |first36=Paul A. |title=The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XIV. A temperate ($T_\mathrm{eq}\sim 300$ K) super-earth around the nearby star Gliese 411 |journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics |volume=A17 |pages=625 |date=15 February 2019 |arxiv=1902.06004 |s2cid=85529763 }}</ref> The proposed planet on 12-day orbit was confirmed by [[CARMENES]] (Calar Alto high-Resolution search for M dwarfs with Exoearths with Near-infrared and optical Echelle Spectrographs) project in 2020.<ref name=Stock2020>{{citation |arxiv=2010.00474 |title=The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs Three temperate-to-warm super-Earths |year=2020 |doi=10.1051/0004-6361/202038820 |last1=Stock |first1=S. |last2=Nagel |first2=E. |last3=Kemmer |first3=J. |last4=Passegger |first4=V. M. |last5=Reffert |first5=S. |last6=Quirrenbach |first6=A. |last7=Caballero |first7=J. A. |last8=Czesla |first8=S. |last9=Béjar |first9=V. J. S. |last10=Cardona |first10=C. |last11=Díez-Alonso |first11=E. |last12=Herrero |first12=E. |last13=Lalitha |first13=S. |last14=Schlecker |first14=M. |last15=Tal-Or |first15=L. |last16=Rodríguez |first16=E. |last17=Rodríguez-López |first17=C. |last18=Ribas |first18=I. |last19=Reiners |first19=Ansgar |last20=Amado |first20=P. J. |last21=Bauer |first21=F. F. |last22=Bluhm |first22=P. |last23=Cortés-Contreras |first23=M. |last24=González-Cuesta |first24=L. |last25=Dreizler |first25=S. |last26=Hatzes |first26=A. P. |last27=Henning |first27=Th. |last28=Jeffers |first28=S. V. |last29=Kaminski |first29=A. |last30=Kürster |first30=Martin |journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics |volume=A112 |page=643 |bibcode=2020A&A...643A.112S |s2cid=222090233 |display-authors=1 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

A second planet with a more distant orbit was initially noticed by SOPHIE, but the baseline was not long enough to confirm the several-year-long signal. The signal was confirmed in 2021 to be a planet with mass at least  {{val|18.0|2.9|2.6|u=M+}},<ref name="Fulton2021">{{cite journal |last1=Rosenthal |first1=Lee J. |last2=Fulton |first2=Benjamin J. |last3=Hirsch |first3=Lea A. |last4=Isaacson |first4=Howard T. |last5=Howard |first5=Andrew W. |last6=Dedrick |first6=Cayla M. |last7=Sherstyuk |first7=Ilya A. |last8=Blunt |first8=Sarah C. |last9=Petigura |first9=Erik A. |last10=Knutson |first10=Heather A. |last11=Behmard |first11=Aida |last12=Chontos |first12=Ashley |last13=Crepp |first13=Justin R. |last14=Crossfield |first14=Ian J. M. |last15=Dalba |first15=Paul A. |last16=Fischer |first16=Debra A. |last17=Henry |first17=Gregory W. |last18=Kane |first18=Stephen R. |last19=Kosiarek |first19=Molly |last20=Marcy |first20=Geoffrey W. |last21=Rubenzahl |first21=Ryan A. |last22=Weiss |first22=Lauren M. |last23=Wright |first23=Jason T. |title=The California Legacy Survey. I. A Catalog of 178 Planets from Precision Radial Velocity Monitoring of 719 Nearby Stars over Three Decades |journal=The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series |date=1 July 2021 |volume=255 |issue=1 |pages=8 |doi=10.3847/1538-4365/abe23c|arxiv=2105.11583|bibcode=2021ApJS..255....8R |s2cid=235186973 |url=https://authors.library.caltech.edu/109839/1/Rosenthal_2021_ApJS_255_8.pdf |doi-access=free }}</ref> a lower-bound estimate later revised to {{val|14.2|1.8|u=M+}}.<ref name=Hurt2021/>

A third planet, Gliese 411 d, is suspected to orbit between Gliese 411 b and Gliese 411 c with a period of 215 days.<ref name=Hurt2021/>

