The [[habitable zone]] for this star, defined as the locations where liquid water could be present on an Earth-like planet, is at a radius of 0.11–0.24&nbsp;[[Astronomical Units|AU]], where 1&nbsp;AU is the average distance from the Earth to the Sun.<ref name="aaa555_A104"/> The planet b has an equilibrium temperature of 370.1{{±|5.8|6.8}} [[Kelvin|K]]. Other known planets are outside HZ boundaries too, but undetected low-mass ones may be orbiting in this region of this system as well.<ref name=Stock2020/>

===Past claims of planets===
Dutch astronomer [[Peter van de Kamp]] wrote in 1945 that Lalande 21185 possessed an "unseen companion" of {{Solar mass|0.06|link=yes}} (about {{Jupiter mass|60|link=yes}}).<ref>{{cite journal | last=van de Kamp | first=Peter | title=Stars Nearer than Five Parsecs | journal=[[Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific]] | volume=57 | issue=334 | pages=34–41 (34, 36, 39) | date=February 1945 | bibcode=1945PASP...57...34V | doi=10.1086/125679 | doi-access=free }}</ref> In 1951 [[Peter van de Kamp|van de Kamp]] and his student [[Sarah Lee Lippincott|Sarah Lippincott]] claimed the [[Methods of detecting extrasolar planets#Astrometry|astrometric detection]] of a planetary system using photographic plates taken with the {{convert|24|in|adj=on}} [[refractor telescope]] at [[Swarthmore College]]'s [[Sproul Observatory]].<ref>{{cite journal
| title=Astrometric study of Lalande 21185
| author1=van de Kamp, P.  |author2=Lippincott, S. L.
| journal= [[The Astronomical Journal]]
| volume=56
| pages=49–50
| date=April 1951
| doi=10.1086/106503 | bibcode=1951AJ.....56...49V| doi-access=free
}}</ref> In the summer of 1960, Sarah Lippincott altered the 1951 claim, to a planet of {{Solar mass|0.01|link=yes}} (that is, {{Jupiter mass|10|link=yes}}), an 8-year orbital period, eccentricity of 0.3, a semi-major axis<ref group=nb>which would be orbital radius if e was 0, that is, a circular orbit</ref> of {{val|0.083|ul=AU}}.<ref>{{cite journal 
| title=Astrometric analysis of Lalande 21185
| author=Lippincott, Sarah Lee
| journal=[[Astronomical Journal]]
| volume=65
| number=7
| pages=445–448
| date=September 1960
| doi=10.1086/108285
| bibcode=1960AJ.....65..445L 
| url=http://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1960AJ.....65..445L | doi-access=free
}}</ref> She used the original photographic plates and new plates taken with the same telescope.<ref>{{cite journal | title=The Unseen Companion of the Fourth Nearest Star, Lalande 21185
| author=Lippincott, Sarah Lee
| journal=[[The Astronomical Journal]]
| volume=65 | pages=350 | date=August 1960 | doi=10.1086/108260 | bibcode=1960AJ.....65..349L}}</ref>  Photographic plates from this observatory, taken at the same time, were used by Van de Kamp for his erroneous claim of a planetary system for [[Barnard's Star]]. The plates made with the Sproul 24-inch refractor and used for these and other studies were in 1973 shown to be flawed;<ref>{{cite journal | author = John L. Hershey |date=June 1973 | title = Astrometric analysis of the field of AC +65 6955 from plates taken with the Sproul 24-inch refractor | journal = Astronomical Journal | volume = 78 | issue = 5 | pages = 421–425 
| bibcode=1973AJ.....78..421H
| doi = 10.1086/111436 | doi-access = free }}</ref> as they were the next year with astrometric measurements made by [[George Gatewood]] of the [[Allegheny Observatory]].<ref>{{cite journal
| title=An astrometric study of Lalande 21185
| author=Gatewood, G. | journal=[[The Astronomical Journal]]
| volume=79 | issue=1 |date=January 1974 | doi=10.1086/111530 
| bibcode=1974AJ.....79...52G
| pages=52}}</ref>

In 1996 the same Gatewood prominently announced at an [[American Astronomical Society|AAS]] meeting<ref name="AAS">{{ cite journal
| title= Lalande 21185
| author=Gatewood, G.
| journal=Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society
| volume=28 | pages=885 | date=May 1996 | bibcode=1996AAS...188.4011G}}</ref> and to the popular press<ref>{{cite news|title=Data Seem to Show a Solar System Nearly in the Neighborhood|newspaper=The New York Times|author=John Wilford|date=1996-06-12|access-date=2009-05-29|page=1|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1996/06/12/us/data-seem-to-show-a-solar-system-nearly-in-the-neighborhood.html}}</ref> the discovery of multiple planets in this system, detected by astrometry. The initial report was based on a very delicate analysis of the star's position over the years, which suggested reflex orbital motion due to one or more companions. Gatewood claimed that such companions would usually appear more than 0.8 arcseconds from the red dwarf itself. Though, a paper by Gatewood published only a few years earlier<ref name="noplanets">{{cite journal
| author= Gatewood
| title=Multichannel astrometric photometer and photographic astrometric studies in the regions of Lalande 21185, BD 56°2966, and HR 4784
| journal=[[The Astronomical Journal]]
| bibcode=1992AJ....104.1237G
| volume=104 | issue=3 | pages=1237–1247 
| date=September 1992
| doi= 10.1086/116313
| last2= Stein
| first2= John
| last3= De Jonge
| first3= Joost K.
| last4= Persinger
| first4= Timothy
| last5= Reiland
| first5= Thomas
| last6= Stephenson
| first6= Bruce| doi-access= free
}}</ref> and later searches by others, using [[coronagraph]]s and multifilter techniques to reduce the scattered-light problems from the star, did not positively identify any such companions,<ref name="nearby">{{cite journal
| title=Photometric and Ca II H and K Spectroscopic Variations in Nearby Sun-like Stars with Planets. III
| url=http://www.iop.org/EJ/article/0004-637X/531/1/415/50376.web.pdf?request-id=b98eea9d-4dc7-462a-9797-bd06712fd0ad
| journal= The Astrophysical Journal
| author=Henry | volume=531
| issue=1 | pages=415–437
| date= March 1, 2000 |  doi=10.1086/308466
| last2=Baliunas
| first2=Sallie L.
| last3=Donahue
| first3=Robert A.
| last4=Fekel
| first4=Francis C.
| last5=Soon
| first5=Willie | bibcode=2000ApJ...531..415H| s2cid=17737644 }}</ref> and so his claim remains unconfirmed and is now in doubt.

Before the 1980s, finding the radial velocity of [[red dwarf]]s was neither very accurate nor consistent, and so due to its apparent brightness and because it does not have a companion, this star, along with eleven other similar red dwarf stars, were chosen to have their [[radial velocity]] measured, to unprecedented high accuracy, by planet hunter [[Geoff Marcy]].<ref name="Marcy1987">{{cite journal
| title=Radial velocities of M dwarf stars
| author=Marcy
| journal=Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific
| volume=99 | date=June 1987 | pages=490–496 | doi=10.1086/132010 
| bibcode=1987PASP...99..490M
| last2=Lindsay
| first2=Victoria
| last3=Wilson
| first3=Karen
| doi-access=free
}}</ref> No companion was detected around this star in this nor other contemporary surveys, and such early equipment would have picked up any planet exceeding {{jupiter mass|0.7}} in an extremely close orbit of 5 days or less; or exceeding {{jupiter mass|10}} at about Jupiter's orbital distance.<ref name="Marcy1987" />

